This approach will catch a large number of true cases of frustration, given the volume, but will likely miss the vast majority of frustrating interactions.

@gabriberton: "The Claude Code leak teaches us that simplicity wins once again. To detect frustration, they literally use a regex that searches for (wtf|shit|...). If you gave that task to almost any PhD student, they'd have trained a 200M 'frustration detection' model."

The linked paper discusses invisible failures in human-AI interactions, highlighting the limitations of simplistic methods like regex for complex emotional detection.

LongCoT is adding two new leaderboards due to the interest in agents, particularly RLMs. The new leaderboards are the 'Restricted Harness' and the 'Open Harness'. The GPT 5.2 RLM from our paper is currently state-of-the-art on the 'Open Harness' at 25.12%, and we expect the tool-use state-of-the-art to exceed this very soon.

On the 'Open Harness', all tool-use and code execution are allowed, while the 'Restricted Harness' permits models to manage context and call subagents but not write specific solver code, such as for BlocksWorld or Sudoku. This leaderboard is exciting because it allows agents to manage their own context while adhering to LongCoT's goal of testing models' intrinsic reasoning capabilities.

@sumeetrm: "We’re releasing LongCoT, an incredibly hard benchmark to measure long-horizon reasoning capabilities over tens to hundreds of thousands of tokens. LongCoT consists of 2.5K questions across chemistry, math, chess, logic, and computer science. Frontier models score less than 10%."

Very cool idea from @OpenAI. I’m really excited about this research preview—learning from how people interact with their computers beyond chat will unlock a ton of really cool interactions.

@OpenAIDevs: "Last week, we released a preview of memories in Codex. Today, we’re expanding the experiment with Chronicle, which improves memories using recent screen context. Now, Codex can help with what you’ve been working on without you restating context."

The linked paper discusses creating general user models from computer use, which relates to understanding user interactions in more depth.

