Pointwise reranking is like single vector embeddings: it’s very fast, ubiquitous, easy to set up, and, unfortunately, increasingly outdated and unsuitable for the needs of modern LLM workflows.

All you need is a strong retriever and a smart reranker. We now have both.

@mixedbreadai introduces mxbai-rerank-v3-listwise, a reranking method that goes beyond binary relevance. It reads the whole candidate set, resolves conflicts, and ranks by directives like recency, source priority, and multi-step rules. It achieves an average of 11% improvement in NDCG@10 across multiple domains, modalities, and languages in runs with Wholembed v3. The feature is available today in preview in Mixedbread.

ICYMI: read the blog on Pedagogical RL. Instead of sampling blindly from your LLM, leverage the label used for RLVR to directly approximate the distribution of plausible rollouts that are actually correct, then sample from that.

@SOURADIPCHAKR18 highlights that typical RL algorithms and on-policy distillation methods are blind samplers, using privileged info to score rollouts but not to find them. The question is whether privileged info can be used to actively sample the rollouts RL aims to discover with compute.

The linked blog introduces Pedagogical RL, which explores this idea of teaching models to teach themselves from privileged information.

